cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "When Validation Changes the Answer: GARCH(1,1) versus Gradient Boosting for Forecasting S&P 500 ETF Volatility"
abstract: >-
  EWMA, GARCH(1,1) and LightGBM forecasts of SPY's 5-day realized variance,
  each scored under a naive random split and under purged expanding-window
  walk-forward validation (26 annual folds, 2000-2025). Under walk-forward
  validation LightGBM's QLIKE advantage over GARCH(1,1) is 0.0124 and not
  significant (Diebold-Mariano p = 0.47); the naive split made it look like
  a decisive winner (p < 0.0001). The failure is traced to the forecast
  ceiling of a tree ensemble during the 2008 crisis.
